\begin_layout Standard
At the heart of the JTAG interface lies a deterministic 16-state finite
 state machine known as the Test Access Port (TAP) controller (see Figure
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:TAP Controller State Machine-1-1"

\end_inset

).
 This controller orchestrates the sequencing and execution of all JTAG operation
s by controlling access to the internal instruction and data registers of
 the target device.
 The TAP controller is central to enabling flexible test and debug functionality
 over a minimal pin interface.
 Its primary states are outlined below z:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Test-Logic-Reset:
\series default
\emph default
 This state asynchronously initializes all JTAG logic within the device,
 ensuring that the system enters a well-defined, inactive configuration.
 It is commonly used to recover from an unknown or unstable state before
 initiating a test or debug session.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Run-Test/Idle:
\series default
\emph default
 After reset, the TAP enters this state, which either maintains the system
 in a passive idle condition or triggers the execution of specific test
 operations, such as Built-In Self-Test (BIST) or continuous scan testing,
 depending on the configuration of the active instruction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Select-DR-Scan / Select-IR-Scan: 
\series default
\emph default
These intermediate states determine the type of register path—
\emph on
Data Register (DR)
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
Instruction Register (IR)
\emph default
—that will be accessed next.
 They serve as branch points for directing the TAP controller’s flow toward
 the desired scan operation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Capture, Shift, Pause, Update States:
\series default
\emph default
 These sets of states exist for both 
\emph on
DR
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
IR
\emph default
 scan paths and are responsible for the detailed handling of serial data.
 The Capture state samples current values into the respective shift register;
 Shift allows for serial movement of bits through the register via 
\emph on
TDI
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
TDO
\emph default
; Pause temporarily halts shifting to allow external processes or decision-makin
g; and Update writes the shifted data into the active register, committing
 the operation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
State transitions within the TAP controller are governed by the 
\emph on
Test Mode Select (TMS)
\emph default
 signal, which is evaluated on the rising edge of the 
\emph on
Test Clock (TCK)
\emph default
.
 This serialized control scheme enables deterministic navigation through
 the TAP state machine using a single control line, eliminating the need
 for parallel signaling or complex external logic.
 Such a mechanism ensures precise coordination of test operations and register
 access.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Instruction Register
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
Instruction Register
\emph default
 (
\emph on
IR
\emph default
) defines the operational context of the JTAG interface by determining which
 data register is accessible during a given operation.
 Typically 5 bits wide, the 
\emph on
IR
\emph default
 is loaded serially via the Shift-IR and Update-IR states within the TAP
 controller.
 Standardized instructions include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
EXTEST: 
\series default
\emph default
Enables external pin-level testing through the boundary scan register.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
SAMPLE/PRELOAD: 
\series default
\emph default
Captures the real-time state of selected signals or preloads values into
 scan chains for subsequent operations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
IDCODE (optional):
\series default
\emph default
 Provides a static device identifier to facilitate device recognition and
 configuration.
 If the identification register is not implemented in hardware, the 
\emph on
IDCODE
\emph default
 instruction must be interpreted as a 
\emph on
BYPASS
\emph default
 instruction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
BYPASS: 
\series default
\emph default
Selects a minimal 1-bit data register, allowing the device to be bypassed
 in a scan chain and enabling faster access to downstream devices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
USER Instructions:
\series default
\emph default
 Reserved opcodes that support vendor-defined or application-specific functional
ity, enhancing the flexibility of the JTAG interface.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The support for 
\emph on
USER
\emph default
 instructions significantly extends the utility of the 
\emph on
IR
\emph default
 by enabling custom debug features and facilitating proprietary extensions
 within standardized test workflows.
\end_layout

