\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V Debug Specification defines a modular and transport-agnostic
 framework for external debuggers to interface with RISC-V processors.
 Its architecture separates the transport, control, and execution layers
 to enable scalable and flexible debug support.
 The primary components include the 
\emph on
Debug Transport Module (DTM)
\emph default
, the 
\emph on
Debug Module Interface (DMI)
\emph default
, and the 
\emph on
Debug Module (DM)
\emph default
—each fulfilling a distinct role in the debug pathway.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Debug communication typically begins with a debugger host (e.g., 
\emph on
GDB
\emph default
) interfacing through a hardware driver (e.g., 
\emph on
OpenOCD
\emph default
), which communicates with the 
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
 using a physical transport such as JTAG or UART.
 The 
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
 forwards these transactions to the 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 via the 
\emph on
DMI
\emph default
, which exposes a set of control and status registers that allow register-level
 interaction with the processor core (hart).
 This architecture supports both single and multi-hart systems, enabling
 robust and extensible debugging functionality (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:RISC-V Debug System Overview-1"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug System Components
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V debug architecture is modular, consisting of three primary components
: the 
\emph on
Debug Transport Module (DTM)
\emph default
, the 
\emph on
Debug Module Interface (DMI)
\emph default
, and the 
\emph on
Debug Module (DM)
\emph default
.
 These components work in conjunction to facilitate interaction between
 an external debugger and the internal state of one or more RISC-V harts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Debug Transport Module (DTM
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "DTM"
description "Debug Transport Module"
literal "true"

\end_inset

):
\series default
 
\emph default
Acts as the physical access point for an external debugger.
 It implements a protocol-specific interface—typically JTAG or Serial Wire
 Debug (SWD)—and forwards incoming debug transactions to the Debug Module
 through the 
\emph on
DMI
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Debug Module Interface (DMI
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "DMI"
description "Debug Module Interface"
literal "true"

\end_inset

):
\emph default
 
\series default
Serves as a memory-mapped interface between the 
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
 and the 
\emph on
Debug Module
\emph default
.
 It supports 32-bit read and write transactions to a defined address space,
 enabling command and status exchange.
 The interface is intentionally minimalistic to ensure lightweight integration
 with transport mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Debug Module (DM):
\series default
\emph default
 Implements the primary debugging functionality.
 It controls hart execution (
\emph on
halt
\emph default
, 
\emph on
resume
\emph default
), provides abstract access to memory and registers, and supports programmable
 instruction execution using a program buffer.
 A single 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 typically serves all harts in a system, allowing fine-grained control via
 internal hart selection mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each hart (hardware thread) is associated with one 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
.
 In multi-hart systems, a centralized 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 facilitates debugging across all harts, simplifying design and reducing
 overhead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Module Interface (DMI)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
DMI
\emph default
 provides the communication pathway between the JTAG interface (via the
 
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
) and the internal 
\emph on
Debug Module
\emph default
.
 It exposes a 32-bit addressable register space where debug operations can
 be initiated or queried.
 The interface is streamlined, requiring only address and data lines, enabling
 efficient read/write access without additional control handshaking.
 This simplicity supports both hardware and software implementations with
 minimal complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Module Registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Debug Module exposes several memory-mapped registers via 
\emph on
DMI
\emph default
 to configure, control, and monitor debugging operations.
 Key registers include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dmcontrol (0x10): 
\series default
\emph default
Enables the debug module, selects the target hart, and issues halt/resume
 requests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dmstatus (0x11):
\series default
\emph default
 Reflects the current status of all managed harts (e.g., 
\emph on
halted
\emph default
, 
\emph on
running
\emph default
, 
\emph on
unavailable
\emph default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
abstractcs (0x16):
\series default
 
\emph default
Indicates the availability and configuration of abstract command mechanisms,
 such as the program buffer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
command (0x17):
\series default
\emph default
 Encodes abstract commands to be executed by the 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 (e.g., register access).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
abstractauto (0x18):
\emph default
 
\series default
Automates execution of abstract commands and data access patterns.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
data0–dataN (0x04–0x0f):
\emph default
 
\series default
Temporary storage registers for abstract command operands or results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
progbuf0–N (0x20–0x2X):
\emph default
 
\series default
Holds RISC-V instructions to be executed during debug mode using the program
 buffer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additional registers such as 
\emph on
hartinfo
\emph default
, 
\emph on
nextdm
\emph default
, 
\emph on
dmcs2
\emph default
, and implementation-specific extensions can be included to support more
 advanced functionality or multicore management.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Abstract Commands and Program Buffer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Abstract commands provide a flexible, implementation-independent method
 for executing fundamental debug tasks.
 The 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 supports the following command types:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Access Register: 
\series default
\emph default
Read or write general-purpose registers (
\emph on
GPRs
\emph default
) or control/status registers (
\emph on
CSRs
\emph default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Quick Access:
\series default
\emph default
 Perform a rapid sequence of halt, instruction execution, and resume for
 common diagnostics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Access Memory:
\series default
\emph default
 Read or write system memory from the perspective of the target hart.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For more complex operations, a 
\emph on
Program Buffer
\emph default
 allows execution of short RISC-V instruction sequences.
 The buffer typically supports at least two instruction slots and must terminate
 with an 
\emph on
ebreak
\emph default
 (or 
\emph on
c.ebreak
\emph default
) instruction.
 This mechanism allows custom register transfers, 
\emph on
CSR
\emph default
 manipulation, and even limited branching within debug mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hart Debug Control & CSRs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each hart features dedicated control and status registers for managing debug
 state:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dcsr (0x7b0): 
\series default
\emph default
The 
\emph on
Debug Control and Status Register (dcsr)
\emph default
 includes fields indicating the cause of entry into debug mode, the current
 privilege level, single-step enable, and other configuration bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dpc (0x7b1):
\series default
\emph default
 
\emph on
Debug Program Counter
\emph default
 
\emph on
(dpc)
\emph default
 stores the address at which the hart was halted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dscratch0, dscratch1 (0x7b2, 0x7b3):
\series default
 
\emph default
General-purpose 
\emph on
scratch registers
\emph default
 available for temporary storage by the debugger during execution of abstract
 commands or program buffer instructions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These 
\emph on
CSRs
\emph default
 ensure safe and controlled operation of the debugger and allow seamless
 entry and exit from debug mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Mode Operation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A RISC-V hart enters Debug Mode when specific events or conditions occur
 that indicate external or internal debug intervention.
 These conditions include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
External Halt Request:
\series default
\emph default
 The debugger sets the 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
 bit in the 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register via the
\emph on
 Debug Module Interface (DMI)
\emph default
.
 This signal requests that the hart halt at the next instruction boundary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Trigger Events:
\emph default
 
\series default
The hart is configured with hardware breakpoints or watchpoints (via trigger
 modules).
 Upon matching a trigger condition—such as a specific instruction fetch,
 memory access, or address match—the hart automatically transitions to Debug
 Mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Single-Step Execution:
\series default
\emph default
 If the 
\emph on
step
\emph default
 bit is set in the 
\emph on
dcsr
\emph default
 
\emph on
(Debug Control and Status Register)
\emph default
, the hart executes one instruction and then automatically halts and enters
 Debug Mode.
 This is useful for instruction-level stepping during software debugging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once a hart enters Debug Mode, the following changes occur in its execution
 environment:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Instruction Fetch Is Disabled:
\series default
\emph default
 The hart's normal instruction execution pipeline is stalled.
 It does not fetch or retire instructions from the normal program memory,
 effectively freezing the program state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Access to Debug Resources:
\series default
\emph default
 While halted, the Debug Module gains exclusive access to the hart’s internal
 state.
 Abstract commands issued by writing to the 
\emph on
command register
\emph default
 allow the debugger to read or write general-purpose registers (
\emph on
GPRs
\emph default
), control and status registers (
\emph on
CSRs
\emph default
), or 
\emph on
memory
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Execution via Program Buffer:
\series default
\emph default
 A small, fixed-size instruction buffer (
\emph on
progbuf
\emph default
) inside the Debug Module allows the debugger to inject and execute RISC-V
 instructions while the hart is halted.
 The program buffer enables flexible, direct manipulation of the hart state
 (e.g., loading registers from memory, executing computation, etc.).
 For correct behavior, program buffer execution must end with an 
\emph on
ebreak
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
c.ebreak
\emph default
 instruction, which causes control to return to the Debug Module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Preservation of Program State:
\series default
\emph default
 Upon entering Debug Mode, the current value of the 
\emph on
Program Counter (PC)
\emph default
 is saved in the 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
 register.
 The cause of entry is recorded in 
\emph on
dcsr.cause
\emph default
, which can indicate external request, trigger match, single-step, or exception
 during debug execution.
 The 
\emph on
dcsr
\emph default
 also reflects the previous privilege level and various control bits such
 as 
\emph on
step
\emph default
, 
\emph on
ebreakm
\emph default
, 
\emph on
ebreaks
\emph default
, and 
\emph on
ebreaku
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Exiting Debug Mode is initiated by the debugger through the 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register.
 The debugger must clear the 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
 bit and set the 
\emph on
resumereq
\emph default
 bit.
 Once the Debug Module confirms that the hart is ready (via 
\emph on
dmstatus
\emph default
), the hart resumes normal instruction execution from the address stored
 in 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If an invalid instruction is executed in the program buffer, or if an exception
 occurs during abstract command execution, the hart remains in Debug Mode,
 and the corresponding error code is updated in the 
\emph on
cmderr
\emph default
 field of the 
\emph on
abstractcs
\emph default
 register.
 This ensures that faulty debug operations do not unintentionally resume
 program execution or corrupt state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This robust mechanism for entering, managing, and exiting Debug Mode makes
 the RISC-V debug architecture suitable for a wide range of use cases—from
 deeply embedded systems requiring minimal overhead to complex multi-core
 SoCs needing advanced debugging and trace capabilities.
\end_layout

